@title Matchmaking and Game Servers
@subtitle Architecture for engineers joining the live team
@author Backend Services team
@date 2026-09-22

Owned by Backend Services. Reviewed 2026-09-22.

## Overview
Players are matched into 10-player games and placed on the nearest regional server fleet. Each region can run alone if another region fails.

## Architecture
diagram arch dir=LR title="Matchmaking and game servers"
arch: players "Players" user
arch: ge "Global Edge" lb
arch: mm "Matchmaker" svc
arch: alloc "Session Allocator" svc
arch: fleet "Game Server Fleet" svc
arch: cache "Regional Cache" store
arch: pdb "Player Profile Database" db "shared"
arch: lbsvc "Leaderboard Service" svc "shared"
arch: players -> ge : connect
arch: ge -> mm : queue request
arch: mm -> cache : skill ratings
arch: mm -> pdb : load profile
arch: mm -> alloc : match of 10 players
arch: alloc -> fleet : start session
arch: players --> fleet : UDP game traffic
arch: fleet -> lbsvc : match results
arch: group reg "Regional Stack (same in each of 3 regions)" mm, alloc, fleet, cache

!! The Player Profile Database and the Leaderboard Service are the only shared parts. Watch them first in a regional failover.

## Regions and capacity
Three regions: us-east, eu-central and ap-southeast. Each has the same setup.

table = Region,Game servers,Concurrent players; us-east,150,9000; eu-central,150,9000; ap-southeast,150,9000

- Each server runs 6 matches of 10 players, so each region supports 9000 concurrent players.
- Median matchmaking time target: 10 seconds. 95th percentile target: 30 seconds.
- Servers scale up to 400 per region when more than 2000 players are waiting in the queue.
